    summary: "Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour is a WHO grade 4 embryonal tumour defined by biallelic inactivation of SMARCB1 (INI1) or, rarely, SMARCA4, both core subunits of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling complex. It is one of the genetically simplest human cancers, often with no other recurrent mutation, yet it splits into three epigenetic subgroups (ATRT-TYR, ATRT-SHH, ATRT-MYC) with different locations, ages and outcomes. Up to 35% of children carry a germline SMARCB1 or SMARCA4 alteration (NCI PDQ; 26% of those tested in the European registry), known as rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome, which matters for siblings and for the risk of synchronous renal or soft-tissue rhabdoid tumours. Most children are three years old or younger at diagnosis (the median was 29.5 months in the European registry), which limits radiotherapy.\n\nFew children survived until intensive multimodal protocols were adopted. The COG trial ACNS0333 combined maximal resection, induction chemotherapy (including high-dose methotrexate), three cycles of high-dose chemotherapy with autologous stem-cell rescue, and focal radiotherapy adapted to age, and, in 65 evaluable patients, lowered the risk of relapse or death in children under 36 months compared with a historical cohort (hazard ratio 0.43, 95% confidence interval 0.28 to 0.66); four-year event-free and overall survival for the whole cohort were 37% and 43% (JCO 2020). In the European EU-RHAB registry (143 patients from 13 countries, 2009 to 2017; anthracycline-based induction followed by radiotherapy or high-dose chemotherapy) five-year overall survival was 34.7% (Neuro-Oncology 2020); the regimen forms the basis of the SIOPE ATRT01 trial. Extent of resection, age, metastatic disease and subgroup all predict outcome.\n\nThe biology points to therapy: loss of SMARCB1 leaves the PRC2 methyltransferase EZH2 unopposed, and the EZH2 inhibitor tazemetostat, which had FDA accelerated approval for advanced epithelioid sarcoma in patients aged 16 and over, produced responses in 26.3% of 19 children with ATRT in the expansion part of a paediatric phase 1 trial and in none of 20 with rhabdoid tumours outside the brain (registry results, NCT02601937). In the Pediatric MATCH phase 2 arm none of the eight children with ATRT responded and the arm missed its response target (JNCI 2023). Tazemetostat was withdrawn by Ipsen from all its markets in March 2026 after its confirmatory trial found blood cancers as second primary malignancies in 5.7% of treated patients and none in the control arm (Clinical Cancer Research 2026, DOI 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-26-2216), and the FDA lists its approval as withdrawn on 22 June 2026. Other avenues include CDK4/6 inhibition, aurora kinase A inhibitors (alisertib) and the EZHIP-independent dependency on the residual SWI/SNF subunit BRG1. Methylation-based subgrouping is expected to stratify the next generation of trials.",
